===Etymology 2===
From {{m|en|used|}}, past of {{m|en|use||perform habitually}} + {{m|en|to||infinitive marker}}.

====Verb====
{{head|en|verb}}

# {{lb|en|temporal location}} [[formerly|Formerly]], and habitually or repeatedly, but possibly no longer.
#: {{ux|en|I '''used to''' be undecided, but now I’m not so sure.&emsp; {{nowrap|I '''used to''' like that band and I still do.}}&emsp; {{nowrap|I '''used to''' know a guy from the UK who pronounced "mother" without the "r".}}&emsp; {{nowrap|It '''used to''' be me, sitting in that chair.}}&emsp; {{nowrap|There '''used to''' be open fields here. Now it's a shopping mall.}}}}
#* {{RQ:RJfrs AmtrPqr|1|030}}
#*: They burned the old gun that '''used to''' stand in the dark corner up in the garret, close to the stuffed fox that always grinned so fiercely. Perhaps the reason why he seemed in such a ghastly rage was that he did not come by his death fairly. Otherwise his pelt would not have been so perfect. And why else was he put away up there out of sight?—and so magnificent a brush as he had too.
#* {{quote-book|year=1898|author={{w|Winston Churchill (novelist)|Winston Churchill}}
|title={{w|The Celebrity}}|chapter=1
|passage=He '''used to''' drop into my chambers once in a while to smoke, and was first-rate company. When I gave a dinner there was generally a cover laid for him. I liked the man for his own sake, and even had he promised to turn out a celebrity it would have had no weight with me.}}
#* '''1980''', Björn Ulvaeus, Benny Andersson (lyrics), Agnetha Fältskog (lead vocalist), {{w|ABBA}}, {{nowrap|''{{w|The Winner Takes It All}}''}}
#*: But tell me does she kiss like I '''used to''' kiss you? / Does it feel the same, when she calls your name?
#* '''2002''', David I. Grossvogel, ''[http://books.google.com.au/books?id=zG9ZAAAAMAAJ&q=%22used+to%22&dq=%22used+to%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=XaowUe6jN8aqlAWVw4CgDQ&redir_esc=y Didn't You '''Used to''' Be Depardieu?]''.
#* '''2003''', G. E. Kruckeberg, ''[http://books.google.com.au/books?id=vhCGHnRONPQC&dq=%22used+to%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=XaowUe6jN8aqlAWVw4CgDQ&redir_esc=y Things My Daddy '''Used to''' Say]''.

=====Usage notes=====
* With ''did'' as an [[Appendix:Glossary#auxiliary|auxiliary verb]] (as in the negative and interrogative), '''{{m|en|use to}}''' is considered standard, especially in American English.<ref>[https://www.merriam-webster.com/words-at-play/is-it-used-to-or-use-to Merriam-Webster: Is It 'Used To' or 'Use To'?]</ref> In other cases, such as ''I use to go to the fair every year'', it is considered an error, motivated by the near (or exact) homophony of ''use to'' and ''used to''.
* The negative may be formed as ''used to not'' or ''used not to'' (''usedn't to, usen't to''), ''did not used to'' (''didn't used to'').<!-- [https://www.adaptingaccents.com/single-post/didnt-used-to https://www.adaptingaccents.com/single-post/didnt-used-to/], [http://www.dailywritingtips.com/addendum-on-used-to-vs-use-to/], [http://virtuallinguist.typepad.com/the_virtual_linguist/2008/10/snowclone-and-bad-grammar-at-clapham-junction-station.html], [http://www.quora.com/Is-use-to-or-used-to-poor-grammar] -->
* The interrogative is constructed like ''did [subject] used to...?'' or ''used [subject] to...?'', varying by region and era.
* When it is not necessary to include the following verb, in some regions it is usual to use the verb {{m|en|do}} as a stand-in (''he works harder than he used to do''), whereas in others it is usual to use no verb at all (''he works harder than he used to'').

